identical either way.\\n\\n6. Add no top-level key and remove none, in either branch. The 9-field response shape is pinned by two existing tests.\\n\\n### Scope area D \\u2014 call-site predicate\\n\\n7. At the single call site (server.py:2681, inside the `if _view_payload_token_count(full_response) > _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET:` block), compute the signal and pass it by keyword:\\n\\n```python\\nnarrowed_sections = full_response.get(\\\"sections\\\")\\nnarrowed_to_single_section = bool(\\n    (section is not None or sections_filter is not None)\\n    and isinstance(narrowed_sections, dict)\\n    and len(narrowed_sections) == 1\\n)\\nreturn _build_over_budget_view(\\n    result, len(serialised), selector, narrowed_to_single_section=narrowed_to_single_section\\n)\\n```\\n\\n8. Read the signal from `full_response[\\\"sections\\\"]`, NOT from `result.sections`. `_filter_view_sections()` mutates `full_response` in place for the plural `sections=[...]` path but does not mutate `result` \\u2014 using `result.sections` would silently miss the plural-narrowing case.\\n\\n9. Both conjuncts are required. \\\"Caller explicitly requested narrowing\\\" AND \\\"exactly one section remains\\\" together capture AC2's scenario. An un-narrowed response for an item that merely happens to have one section keeps the existing text: that caller has not exhausted `sections=`, so recommending it is not an R7 violation, and no AC requires changing it.\",\"constraints\":\"- Do NOT modify `disclosure_handler.py`, `disclosure_types.py`, `ordinal_mapper.py`, or `content_normalizer.py`. They are reference-only.\\n- Do NOT modify any test file. T2 owns all test changes.\\n- Do NOT name `navigate=<ordinal>` with a concrete ordinal value in either hint. Architect section 2 proves the two section-numbering schemes in this package are non-equivalent; asserting an ordinal here would be a NEW R7 prohibition-3 violation. Naming `navigate=`/`head=`/`skip_tokens=` in prose (as in requirement 5) is fine \\u2014 no 